      "id": "y14uymy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "13.2 Important Motivation Theories :Quick Table"
    },
    {
      "id": "ax2j8pf",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Theory</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Theorist</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>One-line Summary</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hierarchy of Needs</p>",
            "<p>Abraham Maslow</p>",
            "<p>5 needs in pyramid: Physiological → Safety → Social → Esteem → Self-Actualisation; satisfy lower first</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>ERG Theory</p>",
            "<p>Clayton Alderfer</p>",
            "<p>Existence, Relatedness, Growth; simultaneous needs; frustration-regression possible</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Two-Factor / Hygiene-Motivator</p>",
            "<p>Frederick Herzberg</p>",
            "<p>Hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction; motivators create satisfaction; salary is hygiene, not motivator</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Theory X and Theory Y</p>",
            "<p>Douglas McGregor</p>",
            "<p>X: lazy workers need control; Y: self-motivated workers seek responsibility; management philosophy</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Achievement-Affiliation-Power</p>",
            "<p>David McClelland</p>",
            "<p>Three needs; nAch = achievement-oriented; nAff = relationship-oriented; nPow = influence-oriented</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Expectancy Theory (VIE)</p>",
            "<p>Victor Vroom</p>",
            "<p>Motivation = Valence × Instrumentality × Expectancy; outcome value × performance → reward × effort → performance</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Equity Theory</p>",
            "<p>J. Stacy Adams</p>",
            "<p>Compare own input/output ratio to referent; perceived inequity motivates behaviour change</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Goal Setting Theory</p>",
            "<p>Locke and Latham</p>",
            "<p>Specific, challenging goals + feedback = higher performance; goal commitment essential</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Reinforcement Theory</p>",
            "<p>B.F. Skinner</p>",
            "<p>Behaviour shaped by consequences; positive reinforcement most effective; variable ratio strongest</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
